    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. Kalidasa: The Jewel of Sanskrit Poetry</h3>\n<p>Associated with the court of Chandragupta II Vikramaditya at Ujjain, Kalidasa authored 7 immortal works:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Two Epics (Mahakavyas):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Raghuvamsha:</em> Chronology of the Raghu dynasty, from Dilipa, Raghu, Dasharatha to Rama.</li>\n      <li><em>Kumarasambhava:</em> Birth of war god Kartikeya from the union of Shiva and Parvati.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Two Lyrical Poems (Khandakavyas):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Meghaduta (The Cloud Messenger):</em> A Yaksha exiled at Ramagiri sends a message of love to his wife at Alaka via a passing rain cloud.</li>\n      <li><em>Ritusamhara:</em> Poetic description of the six seasons of India.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Three Dramatic Plays (Natakas):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Abhijnanashakuntalam:</em> Story of King Dushyanta and Shakuntala; translated into English by Sir William Jones in 1789, creating a sensation in Europe.</li>\n      <li><em>Malavikagnimitram:</em> Romance of Sunga Prince Agnimitra and Malavika.</li>\n      <li><em>Vikramorvasiyam:</em> Mythological love of King Pururavas and celestial nymph Urvashi.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Other Classical Sanskrit Dramatists & Masterpieces</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Bhasa:</strong> Predates Kalidasa; wrote 13 plays rediscovered by T. Ganapati Sastri in Kerala in 1912, including <em>Svapnavasavadatta</em> (The Dream of Vasavadatta) and <em>Pratimanataka</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sudraka:</strong> Authored <em>Mrichchhakatika</em> ('The Little Clay Cart'), a realistic urban comedy-drama about the impoverished merchant Charudatta and courtesan Vasantasena in Ujjain.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vishakhadatta:</strong> Authored <em>Mudrarakshasa</em> (political intrigue of Chanakya winning over Rakshasa to Chandragupta Maurya) and <em>Devichandraguptam</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bhavabhuti:</strong> Court poet of Yashovarman of Kannauj (8th c.); wrote <em>Uttararamacharita</em> (Rama's later life, celebrated for Karuna rasa) and <em>Malatimadhava</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dandin, Subandhu & Banabhatta:</strong> Great prose masters; Dandin wrote <em>Dashakumaracharita</em>; Banabhatta wrote <em>Kadambari</em> (world's earliest romantic novel) and <em>Harshacharita</em>.</li>\n</ul>",
