    "title": "Ancient Tamil Sangam Literature: Tolkappiyam, Eight Anthologies & Epics (Silappadikaram)",
    "subtitle": "Three Sangams, Madurai, Agattiyam, Tolkappiyam, Ettutokai, Pattupattu, Silappadikaram & Manimekalai",
    "learningObjective": "Understand the chronology, poetic conventions, landscape classifications, and epic narratives of ancient Tamil Sangam literature.",
    "introduction": "Sangam literature represents the earliest body of literature in South India, composed in Old Tamil between c. 300 BCE and 300 CE. According to Tamil tradition, three successive literary assemblies (Sangams / Muchangam) were patronized by the Pandya kings at Madurai. The literature is celebrated for its secular realism, dividing human life into <em>Agam</em> (inner love poetry linked to five physiographic landscapes or Tinais) and <em>Puram</em> (outer public heroic poetry of war, kings, and ethics).",
    "detailedExplanation": "<h3>1. The Three Sangams & Tolkappiyam</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>First Sangam (Mudhal Sangam):</strong> Believed to have sat at Old Madurai (submerged by the sea); chaired by Sage Agastya; all texts lost.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Sangam (Idai Sangam):</strong> Sat at Kapatapuram (also swallowed by sea); the only surviving text is <strong>Tolkappiyam</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Sangam (Kadai Sangam):</strong> Sat at modern Madurai; produced the surviving corpus of <em>Ettutokai</em> and <em>Pattupattu</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tolkappiyam:</strong> Authored by <strong>Tolkappiyar</strong>; the oldest extant Tamil work; a comprehensive grammatical and poetic treatise on phonology (Ezhuttu), morphology/syntax (Sol), and poetic conventions/social life (Porul).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Classification of the Sangam Corpus</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ettutokai (Eight Anthologies):</strong> Includes <em>Akananuru</em>, <em>Purananuru</em> (heroic poems on royal valor and death), <em>Kuruntokai</em>, and <em>Narrinai</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pattupattu (Ten Idylls):</strong> Long narrative poems including <em>Tirumurukarruppatai</em> (hymn to Murugan) and <em>Maduraikkanchi</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Five Tinais (Ecological Landscapes):</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li><em>Kurinji (Hills/Mountains):</em> Presiding deity Murugan; theme: clandestine lovers' union.</li>\n      <li><em>Mullai (Pastoral/Forests):</em> Presiding deity Mayon (Vishnu); theme: patient waiting of wife.</li>\n      <li><em>Marudam (Agrarian Plains):</em> Presiding deity Vendan (Indra); theme: domestic lover's quarrel.</li>\n      <li><em>Neydal (Seashore/Coast):</em> Presiding deity Varunan; theme: grief of separation.</li>\n      <li><em>Palai (Arid Desert/Wasteland):</em> Presiding deity Korravai (Durga); theme: dangerous travel and parting.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Tirukkural:</strong> Authored by Saint-poet <strong>Tiruvalluvar</strong>; collection of 1,330 couplets (Kurals) divided into Aram (virtue/Dharma), Porul (wealth/Artha), and Inbam (love/Kama). Revered as the 'Tamil Veda'.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. The Great Tamil Epics</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Silappadikaram ('The Tale of an Anklet'):</strong> Composed by Jain ascetic prince <strong>Ilango Adigal</strong>; narrates the tragic story of merchant Kovalan, his devoted wife <strong>Kannagi</strong>, and courtesan Madhavi. When Kovalan is wrongfully executed by the Pandya King of Madurai for stealing the queen's anklet (Silambu), Kannagi proves his innocence with her gem-filled anklet and curses Madurai, burning the city to ashes. Origin of the <strong>Pattini Cult</strong> (worship of the chaste wife).</li>\n  <li><strong>Manimekalai:</strong> Authored by Buddhist grain-merchant <strong>Sittalai Sattanar</strong>; sequel to Silappadikaram following Kovalan and Madhavi's daughter Manimekalai, who becomes a Buddhist nun and feeds the hungry with the divine inexhaustible begging bowl (Amrita Surabhi).</li>\n</ul>",
